[BlackTeamCountry "ENG"]

{The Oxford Review, 4 Nov 1885. "This was played on March 22nd, 1877, at the St. George's Chess Club in the Inter-University Chess match between Mr, C. Tracey (Lincoln), the Oxford President, and Mr. W. H. Gunston (St. John's College, Cambridge)."} 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. d3 Bc5 {This move is banned by modern authorities, but preferred by Morphy and Lowenthal.} 6. O-O d6 7. Bxc6+ (7. c3 {is now generally favoured.}) 7... bxc6 8. c3 Bg4 9. Bg5 O-O 10. Nbd2 Qd7 11. Nb3 (11. Qc2 {looks a better attack.}) 11... Bb6 12. Bxf6 gxf6 13. Re1 Kh8 14. d4 Rg8 15. Qe2 (15. Qd3 {is better}) 15... Rae8 (15... Rg7 {, so as to double the rooks, is the better continuation.}) 16. Rad1 Qe6 17. Kh1 Rg6 18. Qe3 Reg8 19. Nh4 Rg5 20. f3 Rh5 {Unsafe, for White might have won a piece by Qf2.} 21. g3 Bh3 22. Qe2 Rhg5 (22... Rxh4 {is obviously bad policy.}) 23. Qxa6 f5 24. exf5 (24. Nxf5 {Taking with the Kt and bringing back the Queen to d3 is preferable.}) 24... Bxf5 25. f4 {Dangerous} Qd5+ 26. Ng2 exf4 (26... Bh3 {Before taking the pawn would have been speedier.}) 27. c4 Qf3 28. Rf1 Qe2 29. Rde1 Qc2 30. Rxf4 Bh3 31. Ne3 Qg6 {Threatening ...Rxg3 and preventing White's c4-c5} 32. Rg1 Qd3 33. Nd1 (33. Nf1 {would have been better. The retreat to d1 is fatal.}) 33... Bg4 34. Nf2 Bf3+ 35. Rxf3 Qxf3+ 36. Rg2 Re8 (36... Rxg3 {is unquestionably stronger.}) 37. Nh3 Re1+ 38. Ng1 Rf5 {"and Black wins." Source: The Oxford Review, 4 November 1885, annotations by J M Walker. Contributed by Tim Harding, 2 September 2023. (Corrected 19 February 2026)} 0-1

